// What actually executes across the estate: one row per distinct binary - file, version,
// claimed publisher and hash - with how widely and how recently it ran.
//
// Answers "which version of X do we have", "what started running this week", and "what runs
// from somewhere it should not".
//
// SIGNER IS A CLAIM, NOT A SIGNATURE. ProcessVersionInfoCompanyName comes from the PE version
// RESOURCE, which any author can set to any string - malware claiming "Microsoft Corporation"
// costs nothing and is common. An empty Signer is therefore NOT evidence a file is unsigned,
// and a populated one is NOT evidence it is signed. For the actual Authenticode state join
// DeviceFileCertificateInfo on SHA256; this column only tells you what the file says about
// itself.
//
// IDENTITY IS THE HASH, NOT THE NAME. The same FileName routinely covers several builds -
// measured, 7zG.exe appeared as five distinct SHA256s across five versions in thirty days.
// Grouping by name alone merges them and makes "which version" unanswerable, so SHA256 is part
// of the key.
//
// THE SAME FILE SPLITS INTO SEVERAL ROWS, and it is the path that does it. Windows reports 8.3
// short paths and inconsistent casing, so one program appeared three ways in one window -
// DemoPilot-Live.exe, DEMOPI~1.EXE and demopilot-live.exe. Any count per FileName or
// FolderPath is therefore a FLOOR, not a total. Group on SHA256 when the number has to be
// right.
//
// FileName != OriginalFileName IS WORTH LOOKING AT. OriginalFileName is baked into the version
// resource at build time, so a mismatch means the binary was renamed after it was compiled -
// routine for installers and updaters, and also how a renamed system tool hides. Measured over
// thirty days: 418,271 of 11,012,759 process events carried a mismatch.
//
// "NOT RENAMED" AND "CANNOT TELL" ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS and Renamed keeps them apart. Barely
// half the events carry an OriginalFileName at all - measured, 5,434,686 of 11,012,759 - and
// reporting the other half as "no" would claim a check was done that was not. Renamed is "yes"
// on a mismatch, "no" on a match, and EMPTY when the file declares no original name.
//
// Renamed is a STRING on purpose. A KQL bool arrives through this API as Int64 0/1, so
// -eq 'True' silently matches nothing - which is how a first pass here reported zero renamed
// binaries out of 418,271.
//
// THE EXTENSION IS STRIPPED FROM BOTH SIDES BEFORE COMPARING, because Microsoft's own version
// resources routinely omit it: UsoClient.exe declares "UsoClient", wermgr.exe declares
// "WerMgr". Comparing the raw strings reported 13,523 "renamed" binaries led by ordinary
// Windows components - a list long enough to look alarming and useless enough to waste the
// afternoon that chased it. Only a difference in the BASE name counts.
//
// NO TIME FILTER AND NO DEVICE FILTER. The window is sent as its own timespan, and -Days
// DEFAULTS TO SEVEN - pass it explicitly. DeviceName is deliberately not a column here: this
// is an inventory, and one row per binary per device would be a different, much larger query.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where isnotempty(FileName)
| extend Signer       = tostring(ProcessVersionInfoCompanyName),
         Version      = tostring(ProcessVersionInfoProductVersion),
         OriginalName = tostring(ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName)
| summarize
    Runs      = count(),
    Devices   = dcount(DeviceName),
    Accounts  = dcount(AccountName),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    LastSeen  = max(Timestamp)
    by FileName, Version, Signer, OriginalName, FolderPath, SHA256
// Extensions stripped from BOTH sides first - see the header.
| extend FnBase   = trim_end(@"\.[a-z0-9]+$", tolower(FileName)),
         OrigBase = trim_end(@"\.[a-z0-9]+$", tolower(OriginalName))
| extend Renamed = case(isempty(OriginalName), "",
                        OrigBase != FnBase, "yes",
                        "no")
| project FileName, Version, Signer, Renamed, OriginalName,
          Runs, Devices, Accounts, FirstSeen, LastSeen, FolderPath, SHA256
| order by Runs desc
